TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive reorganization (during player absence)",
    "structural influence (L)",
    "points per possession (PPP) change",
    "absence spell",
    "play-type mix displacement",
    "offensive adaptation"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to operationalize 'offensive reorganization relative to L' (e.g., change in structural fingerprint vs. displacement D_real)?",
    "How to define the baseline and absence windows for computing PPP change?",
    "How to control for confounders such as opponent defensive strength, rest, and concurrent injuries?",
    "How to ensure temporal ordering (reorganization measured before the outcome PPP change within the absence spell) to avoid reverse causality?",
    "How to handle the limited number of absence spells (n=148) for a predictive model with multiple covariates?",
    "How to mitigate selection bias from the reasons for L's absence (injury, rest) being related to team performance?",
    "How to avoid regression to the mean if selecting spells based on extreme pre-absence PPP?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence is available to establish any findings, failed approaches, or prior conclusions for this question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question can be operationalized as a regression: change in team PPP (during L's absence minus pre-absence) regressed on a measure of offensive reorganization (e.g., closeness of the absent spell's play-type mix to the pre-absence mix, or the change in the team's structural fingerprint when L's initiations are removed).",
    "The unit of analysis is an absence spell, defined as a contiguous run of missed games by a player with high structural influence (L).",
    "With 148 spells, power is adequate to detect moderate-to-large associations (e.g., |r| > 0.23) but may be insufficient for smaller effects or complex non-linear relationships.",
    "The predictor 'reorganization relative to L' likely refers to the degree to which the offense changes in response to the removal of L's specific initiation patterns, which can be quantified through the project's displacement or structural influence metrics.",
    "PPP change is noisy at the game level; aggregation over the full absence spell and possibly a longer pre-absence window will improve reliability.",
    "Confounding by opponent quality and schedule strength is a major concern; failing to adjust could produce spurious associations.",
    "If L's absence is due to injury on a struggling team, the predictor-outcome relationship may be confounded by the team's underlying decline."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise meaning of 'relative to L' is ambiguous: it could mean relative to L's own structural influence score, or relative to the team's offense when L is present.",
    "The direction of the predicted relationship is not stated: does greater reorganization predict a smaller PPP drop (better adaptation) or a larger drop (dysfunction from deviation)?",
    "It is unclear whether the 148 spells refer only to absences of high-L players or all absences; if low-L players are included, the exposure (reorganization) may have minimal variance, reducing power.",
    "The minimum absence length for inclusion is not specified; short absences (1-2 games) may not provide stable estimates of reorganization or PPP change.",
    "PPP change may be confounded by the quality of the replacement player, which is not directly in the research question."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No prior research records were retrieved, so the decision is based entirely on the current assessment of feasibility, data characteristics, and methodological risks."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.45,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "148 absence spells may not provide enough power to detect modest associations, especially after controlling for multiple confounders."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent quality, schedule, rest, concurrent injuries, and the reason for L's absence are strong potential confounders of the relationship between reorganization and PPP change."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Both offensive reorganization (derived from play-type classification) and PPP change (noisy, especially over small samples) may have low reliability, attenuating any true association."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "If L is narrowly defined (e.g., only top structural-influence players), the effective number of spells may be much smaller than 148."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "If absence spells are sampled from teams with extreme pre-absence PPP, regression to the mean may create spurious associations with reorganization."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work was identified that addressed this exact question."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Limited number of spells (n=148) constrains the complexity of models and the smallest detectable effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent defensive strength, rest days, home/away, and replacement player quality likely influence both the extent of reorganization and the PPP change."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "PPP is notoriously noisy at the game level; even aggregated over a spell, the signal-to-noise ratio may be low."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "The number of spells with meaningful reorganization may be small if most teams do not change much when L is absent."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "If the reorganization measure uses information from the entire absence spell, and PPP change is defined over the same period, temporal ordering could be violated."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Playing styles and roster construction change across seasons, which may affect the relationship between reorganization and PPP."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.10,
    "rejected": 0.10,
    "untestable": 0.30,
    "artifact": 0.10,
    "uncertain": 0.40
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The expected change in team points per possession (PPP) during a structural influence player's absence, associated with the degree of offensive reorganization (measured as the cosine distance between the pre-absence and absence play-type mix), conditional on pre-absence team PPP, opponent average defensive rating, and rest characteristics.",
    "unit": "One absence spell (contiguous missed games by a single player L).",
    "sample": "All absence spells of length ≥5 games for players with structural influence (L) above the league median, from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular seasons. Exclude spells where the player returned during the spell, and spells overlapping with garbage time exceeding 20% of possessions. N ≈ 148 spells.",
    "power": "Assuming 148 spells, a two-sided alpha of 0.05, and a simple linear regression of PPP change on reorganization (one predictor), the minimum detectable standardized coefficient is approximately 0.23 (80% power). Inclusion of 3-5 covariates will increase this slightly. Power for a correlation of 0.3 is approximately 95%.",
    "controls": "Pre-absence team PPP (over a window equal in length to the spell, ending just before absence), average opponent defensive rating faced during the spell, share of games at home, indicator for back-to-back games, and an indicator for other key starters missing ≥2 games during the spell.",
    "falsification": "1) Placebo test: for each L, select an equal-length window when L was present but another rotation player was absent; test the same reorganization–PPP association (expect null). 2) Temporal misalignment test: define reorganization using only the second half of the spell and PPP change using the first half; if the causal direction is correct, the coefficient should not be larger in this misaligned specification.",
    "multiplicity": "Primary analysis uses a single, pre-registered measure of reorganization (play-type mix cosine distance) and a single outcome window. If multiple operationalizations are tested (e.g., different distance metrics or window lengths), all are reported with a hierarchical testing procedure and adjusted p-values.",
    "decision_rule": "The null hypothesis of no association (β_reorganization = 0) is rejected with a two-sided p < 0.05 from the primary model. Robust standard errors are used. Sensitivity analyses include leave-one-out, exclusion of spells with extreme PPP changes, and alternative reorganization measures.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API play-by-play, processed into possession-level records by the project’s classifier as of 2026-11-01, with derived play-type mixes and structural influence scores on that date."
  }
}

